
// **********            Complete Case Analysis (CCA)          ***********

Problem: Real data has missing values (NaN). Most ML models can't train on them, so we must handle them first.

Two options:
-> Remove the missing data (CCA)
-> Impute (fill) the missing values
     -> Univariate: uses only that column (mean, median, mode...)
     -> Multivariate: uses other columns too (KNN, iterative)

Definition: Complete Case Analysis (list-wise deletion) deletes every row that has a missing value in any column. Only the complete rows stay.

When to use:
-> Data is Missing Completely At Random (no pattern, not related to other columns)
-> Missing part is small, less than 5% of the data

Advantages:
-> Simple and fast
-> Doesn't change the distribution of the remaining data (if missing is random)

Disadvantages:
-> We lose data, too many rows gone means too much information lost
-> Biased result if data is not missing at random
-> Can't handle missing values in new data in production

How to check it is safe:
-> Find % of missing in each column
-> Delete the rows, then compare the distribution before and after. If similar, CCA is safe.

Code:
df.isnull().mean() * 100                                          # % missing per column

cols = [var for var in df.columns if 0 < df[var].isnull().mean() < 0.05]
new_df = df[cols].dropna()                                        # the CCA step

df["Age"].plot.density()                                          # before
new_df["Age"].plot.density()                                      # after

df["city"].value_counts(normalize=True)                           # categorical: compare proportions
new_df["city"].value_counts(normalize=True)

Remember: CCA = dropna(), the checks decide if it is safe


// **********            Handling Missing Numerical Data (Simple Imputer)          ***********

Problem: Deleting rows (CCA) loses data or causes bias when too much is missing or it is not missing at random. So we fill the missing values instead.

Definition: Univariate imputation fills the missing values of a column using only that column's own data.

3 Techniques:
-> Mean / Median: fill with mean (normal data) or median (skewed data or outliers)
     Use when: missing completely at random and less than 5% missing
-> Arbitrary Value: fill with a fixed value like 0, -1, 999 that stands out from real data
     Use when: not missing at random, the model can learn "this value means missing"
-> End of Distribution: fill with a value at the far end of the data
     Normal data: mean + 3 * std
     Skewed data: Q3 + 1.5 * IQR
     Use when: not missing at random (same as arbitrary, but value is picked automatically)

Important points:
-> Mean/median changes the shape and variance (values pile up at the mean/median) and reduces correlation with other columns.
-> All three learn a value from data, so fit only on train data, transform on both train and test, otherwise it is DATA LEAKAGE.
-> SimpleImputer handles mean, median and constant. End of distribution is done by hand.

Code:
from sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer

imp = SimpleImputer(strategy="median")                       # "mean" or "median"
X_train_t = imp.fit_transform(X_train[["Age"]])
X_test_t = imp.transform(X_test[["Age"]])

imp = SimpleImputer(strategy="constant", fill_value=-1)      # arbitrary value

extreme = X_train["Age"].mean() + 3 * X_train["Age"].std()   # end of distribution
X_train["Age_end"] = X_train["Age"].fillna(extreme)
X_test["Age_end"] = X_test["Age"].fillna(extreme)

Remember:
-> Missing at random and small = mean or median
-> Missing has a meaning = arbitrary value or end of distribution

// **********            Handling Missing Categorical Data          ***********

Problem: Mean and median only work on numbers. A text column (City, Embarked) needs a different way to fill the missing values.

Definition: Categorical imputation fills the missing values of a text column using that column's own data.

2 Methods:
-> Most Frequent (Mode): fill with the category that appears most often
     Ex: S, S, C, NaN, S -> NaN becomes S
     Use when: missing at random and less than 5% missing
-> Missing Category: fill with a new category called "Missing"
     Ex: S, S, C, NaN, S -> NaN becomes Missing
     Use when: a lot is missing, or missing values may carry meaning

Advantages and Disadvantages:
-> Mode: simple and fast, but if many values are missing the most frequent category becomes too big (distorts data, adds bias)
-> Missing Category: no assumption and the model can learn "missing" as a pattern, but if very few values are missing it creates a tiny useless category

Important points:
-> Mode learns a value from data, so fit only on train data, transform on both train and test, otherwise it is DATA LEAKAGE.
-> After imputing, the column is still text, so encode it next.

Code:
from sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer

imp = SimpleImputer(strategy="most_frequent")                         # mode
X_train_t = imp.fit_transform(X_train[["Embarked"]])
X_test_t = imp.transform(X_test[["Embarked"]])

imp = SimpleImputer(strategy="constant", fill_value="Missing")        # missing category

Remember:
-> Small and random = mode
-> Many missing or has meaning = Missing category

// **********            Random Sample Imputation and Missing Indicator          ***********

Problem: Mean/median imputation squeezes many values to one point, so the shape and variance of the column change. Also, once a value is filled, the model no longer knows it was missing.

// Random Sample Imputation
Definition: Fill each missing value with a randomly picked value from the column's own observed (non-missing) values. Works for numerical and categorical columns.
Ex: Age 22, 25, 28, NaN, 30 -> NaN becomes a random pick from 22, 25, 28, 30.

Why: Keeps the distribution (mean, variance) close to the original.

Use when: missing completely at random.

Disadvantages:
-> Randomness, result changes each run unless random_state is fixed
-> Training data must stay available to pick values from, even in production
-> Doesn't keep the relationship with other columns

Code:
observed = X_train["Age"].dropna()                         # observed values, train only
n_train = X_train["Age"].isnull().sum()

X_train["Age_imputed"] = X_train["Age"]                    # copy, fill only the copy
X_train.loc[X_train["Age"].isnull(), "Age_imputed"] = observed.sample(n_train, random_state=0).values

-> Same for test, but pick from train's observed values (no leakage).
-> .values places the picked values by position, not by index.


// Missing Indicator
Definition: Adds a new 1/0 column showing whether the value was missing (1) or not (0), next to the imputed column.

Why: Keeps the information "this was missing", which the model can learn from when missing values carry meaning.

Important points:
-> Used together with an imputation (mean, median, mode or random), not alone.
-> Adds extra columns.
-> Fit only on train data, transform on both train and test, otherwise it is DATA LEAKAGE.

Code:
X_train["Age_missing"] = X_train["Age"].isnull().astype(int)     # by hand

from sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer
imp = SimpleImputer(strategy="median", add_indicator=True)       # imputed column + indicator column
X_train_t = imp.fit_transform(X_train[["Age"]])
X_test_t = imp.transform(X_test[["Age"]])

Remember:
-> Random imputation = keeps the shape of the data
-> Missing indicator = keeps the "was missing" information


// **********            KNN Imputer (Multivariate Imputation)          ***********

Problem: Mean, median and mode fill a column using only its own values. But a missing value (like Age) may depend on other columns (Fare, Class). Univariate methods ignore that.

Definition: Multivariate imputation fills a missing value using the other columns too. KNN Imputer fills it with the average of the same feature from the k nearest rows in the training data.

How it works:
-> Take a row with a missing value
-> Find the k nearest rows using the other columns (distance)
-> Fill with the average of those neighbours' values
Ex: Row D (Fare 11, Pclass 3, Age NaN) is closest to A (Age 22) and B (Age 24), so Age = 23

Distance: nan-Euclidean distance, normal Euclidean distance using only the columns that are not missing in both rows.

Parameters:
-> n_neighbors: number of neighbours k (default 5)
-> weights: "uniform" (plain average) or "distance" (closer neighbours count more)

Advantages: more accurate than mean/median, because it uses other columns.

Disadvantages:
-> Slow on big data (distance for every row)
-> Training data must be kept, even in production
-> Sensitive to outliers and column scales

Important points:
-> Scale the data first (fit scaler on train only), otherwise big-range columns dominate the distance.
-> Fit only on train data, transform on both train and test, otherwise it is DATA LEAKAGE.
-> Works on numbers only, encode text columns first.
-> Output is a NumPy array, put the column names back with a DataFrame.
-> Only the columns you pass are used, choose columns related to the missing column.

Code:
from sklearn.impute import KNNImputer

scaler = StandardScaler()
X_train_s = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)
X_test_s = scaler.transform(X_test)

knn = KNNImputer(n_neighbors=3, weights="distance")
X_train_i = knn.fit_transform(X_train_s)
X_test_i = knn.transform(X_test_s)

X_train_final = pd.DataFrame(scaler.inverse_transform(X_train_i), columns=X_train.columns)   # back to original scale

# specific columns only
cols = ["Age", "Fare", "Pclass"]
X_train_t = knn.fit_transform(X_train[cols])

Remember: KNN Imputer = fill with the average of the nearest rows
